// An import that is actually checked.
//
// Everything named in the `use` line below is verified against what
// `examples/names.vow` declares. Asking for a name that file does not have is
// VOW3008, and asking for a module that is not among the files handed to the
// compiler is VOW3007. Both used to be accepted in silence, which meant a typo
// in an import produced a working build and a mystery later.
//
// What is still missing is the second half. `Tone` and `Greeting` cross the
// module boundary as names, not as types, so the type checker treats them as
// unknown and an unknown type agrees with everything. That is why `describe`
// below can take a `Greeting` and do nothing useful with it.

module examples/greeting

use examples/names.{Greeting, Loud, Tone}

// The variants come across as names of their own, so importing one is written
// down like anything else. What is not there yet is the type behind them, so a
// `match` on `Tone` cannot be checked for exhaustiveness and writing this as an
// `if` is the honest shape until that lands.
fn describe(tone: Tone) -> String {
    if tone == Loud {
        return "shouting"
    }
    "speaking"
}

// `greeting` is a `Greeting` as far as the reader is concerned and `Unknown` as
// far as the type checker is concerned, so nothing here is verified against
// what `Greeting` actually holds. That gap is the second half of the work.
fn render(greeting: Greeting) -> String {
    "hello"
}

// There is no `test` in this file, and that is the third gap. The interpreter
// holds the code of one module at a time, so `describe(Plain)` has nothing to
// evaluate `Plain` to and reports VOW6006. Checking crosses the boundary now;
// running does not.
